const EN = `# Food and Dining in Vietnam

Vietnamese food is fresh, light, and full of herbs. Meals are often shared, local places are cheap, and good food is easy to find in both big cities and small towns. Dishes, prices, and opening hours can change, so use this guide as a starting point.

---

## Must-Try Dishes
- **Phở:** Rice noodle soup with beef (phở bò) or chicken (phở gà). Many people eat it for breakfast, but you can find it all day.
- **Bún chả:** Grilled pork with rice noodles, fresh herbs, and a sweet and sour dipping sauce. This is a famous dish from Hanoi.
- **Bánh mì:** A crispy baguette sandwich with meat, pickled vegetables, herbs, and sauce. It is a cheap and quick meal.
- **Cơm tấm:** Broken rice served with grilled pork and side dishes. It is very popular in Ho Chi Minh City.
- **Gỏi cuốn:** Fresh rice paper rolls with shrimp, herbs, and noodles, served with a dipping sauce.
- **Chả giò (south) or nem rán (north):** Crispy fried spring rolls.
- **Bánh xèo:** A crispy savory pancake filled with pork, shrimp, and bean sprouts. You wrap pieces in lettuce and herbs.
- **Bún bò Huế:** A spicy beef noodle soup from central Vietnam.
- **Cà phê trứng:** Egg coffee, a sweet and creamy drink that started in Hanoi.

---

## Food in Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City
- **Hanoi (north):** Flavors are usually more gentle and balanced. The city is famous for phở, bún chả, chả cá (fish cooked with dill and turmeric), and egg coffee.
- **Ho Chi Minh City (south):** Flavors are often sweeter, and meals come with more fresh herbs and vegetables. The city is famous for cơm tấm, bánh mì, bánh xèo, and hủ tiếu (a noodle soup with pork).
- Both cities also have dishes from every region of Vietnam, plus Korean, Japanese, Western, and Indian restaurants.

---

## Where to Eat
- **Street food stalls:** Cheap, fast, and often very good. Many stalls cook only one or two dishes, and they cook them well.
- **Local restaurants (quán ăn):** Simple family places with a short menu.
- **Markets and food courts:** A good way to try many dishes in one place.
- **Cafés and restaurants in tourist areas:** Easier if you need an English menu, but usually more expensive.
- Small plastic tables and stools on the sidewalk are normal. Local people eat the same way.
- Choose places that are busy with local customers. A busy place usually means fresh food.

---

## Ordering and Paying
- Many small places have no menu. Look at what other people are eating and point, or use a translation app.
- If prices are not shown, ask the price before you order, especially in tourist areas.
- Cash in Vietnamese dong is the easiest way to pay at street stalls and small shops. Many restaurants and cafés in big cities accept cards or QR payments, but check before you order.
- Tipping is not required. Rounding up the bill or leaving a small amount for good service is welcome in restaurants.
- Some restaurants add a service charge or VAT to the bill. Check the bill before you pay.
- Items placed on your table, such as wet wipes or snacks, may be charged if you use them. You can politely say no.
- To ask for the bill, say **"Tính tiền"**, or catch the staff member's eye and make a small writing gesture.

---

## Useful Phrases
- **Cảm ơn:** Thank you
- **Ngon quá:** Very delicious
- **Không cay:** Not spicy
- **Ít đường:** Less sugar
- **Không đá:** No ice
- **Tôi ăn chay:** I am vegetarian
- **Tính tiền:** The bill, please

---

## Drinks
- **Cà phê sữa đá:** Strong Vietnamese coffee with sweet condensed milk and ice. Ask for less sugar if you do not like very sweet drinks.
- **Cà phê cốt dừa:** Coconut coffee, a creamy and sweet cold drink.
- **Trà đá:** Iced tea that many local restaurants give for free.
- **Nước mía:** Fresh sugarcane juice.
- **Fresh coconut and sinh tố:** Fresh coconut water and fruit smoothies are cheap and easy to find.
- **Bia hơi:** Fresh draft beer, very popular in Hanoi and served in simple street bars. Local bottled beers such as Saigon, 333, and Hanoi Beer are also common.
- **Water:** Do not drink tap water. Use bottled or filtered water. In most restaurants in big cities, the ice is made from treated water. If you have a sensitive stomach, ask for no ice (không đá).

---

## Food Safety
- Choose food that is cooked fresh and served hot.
- Eat at busy places where the food is sold quickly.
- Peel fruit yourself, or wash it with bottled water.
- Fresh herbs and raw vegetables are normal in Vietnam. If your stomach is sensitive, try small amounts first.
- Wash your hands or use hand sanitizer before you eat.
- If you often have stomach problems, bring basic stomach medicine from home. If you feel very sick, go to a clinic or a doctor, and keep your travel insurance details with you.

---

## Vegetarian, Halal, and Allergies
- **Vegetarian:** Look for places with **"quán chay"** or **"cơm chay"** on the sign. Buddhist vegetarian restaurants are common and often cheap. Many regular dishes use meat broth, fish sauce (nước mắm), or shrimp paste, even when they look vegetarian, so say clearly what you cannot eat.
- Many Vietnamese Buddhists eat vegetarian food on the 1st and 15th day of the lunar month, so vegetarian restaurants can be busier on those days.
- **Halal:** Halal restaurants are limited and mostly in big cities. Search on a maps app and ask your hotel for current places.
- **Allergies:** Peanuts, shrimp, fish sauce, and soy are used in many dishes. Show a short allergy note written in Vietnamese (use a translation app) and ask the staff to check the ingredients. Cross-contact is possible at street stalls.
- **Gluten:** Rice noodles are common, but soy sauce, hoisin sauce, and some dipping sauces may contain wheat.

---

## Avoiding Tourist Traps
- Be careful in restaurants near major tourist places that show no prices. Make sure you know the price before you order.
- Some street fruit sellers charge very high prices for fruit or for photos. Agree on the price first.
- Check recent reviews on a maps app, and compare prices at nearby places if you are not sure.
- If you think you were charged too much, stay calm and polite, and ask for a written bill with each item.
- For typical daily costs, see the Budget Tips guide.`

